  "title": "How Practice Changes the Brain",
  "emoji": "🔄",
  "color": "#7E22CE",
  "description": "A short lesson to help you apply How Practice Changes the Brain.",
  "is_free": true,
  "published": true,
  "story": {
    "hook": {
      "text": "Brain scans of professional musicians reveal dramatically larger auditory and motor cortices compared to non-musicians.",
      "visual": "🎻"
    },
    "buildup": {
      "text": "Repeated practice strengthens the synaptic pathways involved in a skill, making signal transmission faster and more reliable.",
      "visual": "🛤️"
    },
    "discovery": {
      "text": "As pathways strengthen, the brain dedicates more cortical territory to the practiced skill, a process called cortical expansion.",
      "visual": "🗺️"
    },
    "twist": {
      "text": "The changes are proportional to hours practiced. Ten thousand hours of violin reshapes the brain more than one thousand.",
      "visual": "📊"
    },
    "climax": {
      "text": "Eventually, well-practiced skills become automatic. The prefrontal cortex steps back and lets specialized circuits run the show.",
      "visual": "🤖"
    },
    "punchline": {
      "text": "Practice does not make perfect. Practice makes permanent brain architecture.",
      "visual": "🏛️"
    }
  },
  "quiz": {
    "question": "What happens to cortical territory with repeated practice?",
    "options": [
      "It expands for the practiced skill",
      "It shrinks to conserve energy",
      "It remains unchanged regardless of practice"
    ],
    "correct": 0
  },
